  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 PM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nA chilly start to the day as temperatures dropped into the lower \nto middle 30s across the north. The cold spot was at the Eagle \nRiver COOP site at 31 degrees. Temperatures rebounded quickly as \ntemperatures early this afternoon climbed into the 60s, with a few\nlower 70s across east-central WI. Clouds were on the increase \nwhile rain was entering western WI as of 1 PM.\n\nFor tonight, a cold front will approach from the west. The 850mb\nlow level jet will increase to 50-60 knots, resulting in 850mb\nwarm advection and will result in the precipitation expanding and\nmoving northeastward into the area later this afternoon and \ncontinuing this evening. Two rounds of heavy rain expected with \nthe first round across northern WI, generally north of Highway 29 \nlate this afternoon and early this evening. The second round of \nheavy rain is expected across central into east-central WI during \nthe late evening and overnight hours. Most unstable CAPE values \nnot impressive with less than 400 J/KG, thus thunderstorm activity\nshould be isolated to scattered across central and east-central. \nThe risk of severe weather is low. Despite strong winds aloft, \nstable layer aloft should prevent much of the winds from mixing \ndown to the surface. Latest model guidance suggest a band of \nheavier rainfall extending from central WI northeast towards \nMarinette County where totals of 1 to 2 inches are expected. Could\nnot rule out some locally higher amounts. The showers and storms \nshould exit the area from northwest to southeast overnight into \nearly Tuesday morning. Dry conditions should prevail through \nWednesday afternoon. \n\nThe baroclinic zone is expected to set up over or just south\nof the forecast area, bringing periodic chances of showers and \nstorms Wednesday night into next weekend. The system for \nFriday into Saturday has the potential to bring heavy rain\nto the area. Temperatures at or above normal expected \nthrough next weekend.",
